## In 30 seconds

Open dialogs through **DialogCommand** with a **DialogWB** wave bean — never block the Model on `Alert.showAndWait()` directly. **DialogDaemonCommand** (boot component) can dispatch `OPEN_DIALOG` waves. Swap implementations via `@Register(DialogCommand.class)`.

Quick Start
---------------------

Add the dialog module. Dialogs open on the JavaFX Application Thread via `DialogCommand`, without blocking your Model logic.

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.jrebirth.af</groupId>
    <artifactId>dialog</artifactId>
    <version>${jrebirth.version}</version>
</dependency>
```

**Minimal example — show an information dialog from a Model:**

```java
@Override
protected void doSave(final Wave wave) {
    final DialogWB wb = new DialogWB();
    wb.setDialogType(DialogWB.DialogType.Info);
    wb.setTitle("Saved");
    wb.setHeader("Operation complete");
    wb.setMessage("Your changes were saved successfully.");
    callCommand(DialogCommand.class, wb);
}
```

`DialogDaemonCommand` is a `@BootComponent` that listens for `OPEN_DIALOG` waves if you prefer wave-based dispatch. `BasicOpenDialogCommand` (JavaFX `Alert`) is the default; register a higher-priority `DialogCommand` to swap implementations.


Dialog
==============


Overview
---------------------

The JRebirth Dialog module provides a **command-based** approach to opening dialogs. Instead of creating and managing dialog instances directly in your Models or Views, you call a Command that handles the dialog lifecycle. This keeps dialog logic separate from UI models and ensures dialogs are displayed on the correct thread (JavaFX Application Thread).

The module supports multiple dialog implementations through the `DialogCommand` registration point, allowing you to swap between basic JavaFX Alert dialogs and third-party libraries like SimpleDialogFX.


Architecture
---------------------

The dialog system is built around these components:

* **DialogCommand** &mdash; the registration point interface defining dialog operations.
* **AbstractDialogCommand** &mdash; base command handling dialog type dispatch.
* **DialogDaemonCommand** &mdash; a boot component that listens for dialog waves and delegates to `DialogCommand`.
* **DialogWB / DialogWaveBean** &mdash; wave beans carrying dialog configuration.
* **BasicOpenDialogCommand** &mdash; default implementation using JavaFX `Alert`.
* **SimpleDialogCommand** &mdash; alternative implementation using SimpleDialogFX library.


DialogCommand Interface
---------------------

**DialogCommand** is the registration point for dialog implementations. It defines three operations corresponding to dialog severity levels:


```java
@RegistrationPoint
public interface DialogCommand extends CommandBean<DialogWB> {

    void openInfoDialog();

    void openWarningDialog();

    void openErrorDialog();

}
```


AbstractDialogCommand
---------------------

**AbstractDialogCommand** is the base class for all dialog command implementations. It runs on the JavaFX Application Thread (`@RunInto(RunType.JAT)`) and dispatches to the appropriate dialog method based on the `DialogType` in the wave bean.


```java
@RunInto(RunType.JAT)
public abstract class AbstractDialogCommand extends AbstractSingleCommand<DialogWB> implements DialogCommand {

    protected DialogWB bean;

    @Override
    protected void initCommand() {
        // Nothing to do yet

    }

    @Override
    protected void perform(Wave wave) throws CommandException {

        bean = waveBean(wave);

        switch (bean.getDialogType()) {

            case Info:
                openInfoDialog();
                break;
            case Warning:
                openWarningDialog();
                break;
            case Error:
                openErrorDialog();
                break;
        }

    }

    @Override
    protected void initInnerComponents() {
        // Nothing to do yet

    }

}
```


DialogDaemonCommand
---------------------

**DialogDaemonCommand** is a `@BootComponent` that starts automatically with the application. It listens for `OPEN_DIALOG` waves on a background thread (`@RunInto(RunType.JIT)`) and delegates to the registered `DialogCommand` implementation. This allows any component to open a dialog by simply sending a wave.


```java
/**
 * The Class DialogDaemonCommand.
 */
@BootComponent
@RunInto(RunType.JIT)
public class DialogDaemonCommand extends AbstractSingleCommand<DialogWB> {

    /** The Constant OPEN_DIALOG_WT. */
    public static final String OPEN_DIALOG_WT = "OPEN_DIALOG";

    /** The open dialog. */
    private static final WaveType OPEN_DIALOG = WBuilder.waveType(OPEN_DIALOG_WT);

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void initCommand() {
        listen(OPEN_DIALOG);
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void perform(Wave wave) throws CommandException {
        callCommand(DialogCommand.class, waveBean(wave));
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void initInnerComponents() {
        // Nothing to do yet

    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    @OnWave(OPEN_DIALOG_WT)
    protected void processWave(final Wave wave) {
        if (OPEN_DIALOG.equals(wave.waveType())) {

            callCommand(DialogCommand.class, waveBean(wave));

        }
    }

}
```


DialogWaveBean
---------------------

**DialogWaveBean** is the `@Bean`-annotated interface defining the data carried by dialog waves. It uses the JRebirth Bean processor to generate an implementation class automatically.

Properties:

* `dialogType()` &mdash; the severity level (Info, Warning, Error).
* `title()` &mdash; the dialog window title.
* `header()` &mdash; the header text.
* `message()` &mdash; the content message.
* `exception()` &mdash; an optional exception for error dialogs.


```java
@Bean
public interface DialogWaveBean extends WaveBean {

    enum DialogType {

        Info,
        Warning,
        Error

    }

    DialogType dialogType();

    String title();

    String header();

    String message();

    Throwable exception();

}
```


BasicOpenDialogCommand
---------------------

**BasicOpenDialogCommand** is the default dialog implementation using standard JavaFX `Alert` dialogs. It is registered with `PriorityLevel.Lowest`, meaning it will be used unless a higher-priority implementation is available.


```java
@Register(value = DialogCommand.class, priority = PriorityLevel.Lowest)
public class BasicOpenDialogCommand extends AbstractDialogCommand {

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    public void openInfoDialog() {
        final Alert alert = new Alert(AlertType.INFORMATION);
        alert.setTitle(bean.getTitle());
        alert.setHeaderText(bean.getHeader());
        alert.setContentText(bean.getMessage());

        alert.showAndWait();
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    public void openWarningDialog() {
        final Alert alert = new Alert(AlertType.WARNING);
        alert.setTitle(bean.getTitle());
        alert.setHeaderText(bean.getHeader());
        alert.setContentText(bean.getMessage());

        alert.showAndWait();
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    public void openErrorDialog() {
        final Alert alert = new Alert(AlertType.ERROR);
        alert.setTitle(bean.getTitle());
        alert.setHeaderText(bean.getHeader());
        alert.setContentText(bean.getMessage());

        alert.showAndWait();
    }
}
```


SimpleDialogCommand
---------------------

**SimpleDialogCommand** provides an alternative implementation using the [SimpleDialogFX](https://github.com/Daytron/SimpleDialogFX) library. It is registered with `PriorityLevel.Low`, giving it higher priority than the basic implementation.


```java
@Register(value = DialogCommand.class, priority = PriorityLevel.Low)
public class SimpleDialogCommand extends AbstractDialogCommand implements DialogCommand {

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    public void openInfoDialog() {
        final Dialog dialog = new Dialog(
                                         DialogType.INFORMATION,
                                         bean.getTitle(),
                                         bean.getHeader(),
                                         bean.getMessage());
        dialog.showAndWait();
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    public void openWarningDialog() {
        final Dialog dialog = new Dialog(
                                         DialogType.WARNING,
                                         bean.getTitle(),
                                         bean.getHeader(),
                                         bean.getMessage());
        dialog.showAndWait();
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    public void openErrorDialog() {
        final Dialog dialog = new Dialog(
                                         DialogType.ERROR,
                                         bean.getTitle(),
                                         bean.getHeader(),
                                         bean.getMessage());
        dialog.showAndWait();
    }

}
```


Usage
---------------------

### Opening a Dialog from Any Component ###

The simplest way to open a dialog is via `callCommand`:

```java
final DialogWB wb = new DialogWB();
wb.setDialogType(DialogWB.DialogType.Info);
wb.setTitle("Information");
wb.setHeader("Operation Complete");
wb.setMessage("The file has been saved successfully.");

callCommand(DialogCommand.class, wb);
```

### Opening a Dialog via Wave ###

You can also send a wave to the `DialogDaemonCommand`:

```java
sendWave(WBuilder.wave()
    .waveType(WBuilder.waveType(DialogDaemonCommand.OPEN_DIALOG_WT))
    .waveBean(wb));
```

### Replacing the Dialog Implementation ###

To provide a custom dialog appearance, create a class extending `AbstractDialogCommand` and register it with a higher priority:

```java
@Register(value = DialogCommand.class, priority = PriorityLevel.Normal)
public class MyCustomDialogCommand extends AbstractDialogCommand {
    // Custom dialog rendering
}
```


